---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: Largs Community Council
heading: Largs Community Council
subtitle: What the Community Council has raised, who is responsible, and what happens next.
permalink: /council/
---
{# Council corner. Two rules are load-bearing on this page and encoded
   below rather than left to memory:
   1. Neutrality — the digest reports what the minutes record, same
      format for every item and every person; names appear exactly as
      the minutes give them; nothing is rated, ranked or scored. The
      official record is always linked. Status describes the RECORD,
      not the world: it says what the minutes say at the last mention.
   2. Honesty about state — while council.sample is true the digest is
      clearly labelled as layout, and unconfirmed meeting details never
      render as fact.
   Status is never carried by colour alone: every one shows a glyph and
   a word, and the key spells all four out.

   The issue card, the status key and the counts strip now live in
   _includes/register.njk, shared with the generated filter pages, so
   the card can never drift between the hub and the register.

   This page shows only the items recorded at the most recent published
   meeting. The register spans a year and would otherwise bury the rest
   of the page; the counts strip carries the reader to the whole of it. #}

{% from "register.njk" import issueCard, statusKey, registerNav, coverageNote %}

{% set latest = council.digest | recordedAt(council.latestMinutes) | newestFirst %}

<section class="board wrap" aria-labelledby="page-title">
  <div class="kicker">
    {% if council.sample %}
    <span class="stamp">Sample layout — first real digest to follow</span>
    {% else %}
    <span class="stamp">{{ council.latestMinutes | monthYear }} digest</span>
    {% endif %}
  </div>

  <p class="page-intro">Two different councils serve Largs, and a lot of wasted
  letters come from mixing them up. <strong>Largs Community Council</strong> is
  volunteers — residents who meet monthly and put the town's case to the
  authorities. <strong>North Ayrshire Council</strong> is the local authority —
  the elected body that runs bins, roads, schools and planning. This page keeps
  track of both, and routes the everyday problems to the right front door.</p>
</section>

{# ================= THE REGISTER ================= #}
<section class="wrap council-page" aria-labelledby="register-h">
  <h2 id="register-h">The issue register</h2>
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <p class="page-intro">Everything the register is keeping track of, by status — the
  newest set of minutes is digested just below. Items carry forward from meeting to meeting
  under a permanent entry, so a matter raised in January can still be found in
  November whether or not it has moved. Nothing is dropped for being slow.</p>

  {{ registerNav(council, "") }}
</section>

{# ================= THE DIGEST ================= #}
<section class="wrap council-page" aria-labelledby="digest-h">
  <h2 id="digest-h">The Community Council digest</h2>
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  {% if council.sample %}
  <p class="sample-note">These entries show the layout only — none of them is
  a real decision. The first real digest follows the Community Council's next
  published minutes.</p>
  {% endif %}

  <div class="mono-h">{{ council.latestMinutes | dateLong }} · what the minutes record · who is responsible · what happens next</div>

  {{ coverageNote(council) }}

  <div class="issues">
    {% for item in latest %}
    {{ issueCard(item, council.minutesUrl, council.latestMinutes) }}
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  {{ statusKey() }}

  <p class="cc-note">How this digest works: it reports what the published
  minutes record — what was decided, who took the action, and what the minutes
  say happens next. A status describes the record and nothing more; it says
  what the minutes said at the last mention, not what may have happened since.
  The same format for every item and every person, names exactly as the minutes
  give them, nothing rated and nothing ranked. The minutes themselves are always
  the official record. <a href="/council/how-this-works/">What goes in the register, and what stays out →</a></p>

  {% if council.minutesUrl %}
  <a class="more" href="{{ council.minutesUrl }}">Read the full minutes →</a>
  {% endif %}
</section>

{# ================= MEETINGS & TAKING PART ================= #}
<section class="wrap council-page" aria-labelledby="meet-h">
  <h2 id="meet-h">Meetings and how to take part</h2>
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  {% if council.meeting.confirmed %}
  <p class="meeting-line">Next meeting: <strong>{{ council.meeting.when }}</strong>{% if council.meeting.venue %}, {{ council.meeting.venue }}{% endif %} <span class="mono">· {{ council.meeting.note }}</span></p>
  {% else %}
  <p class="meeting-line">The next meeting date is being confirmed with the
  Community Council — it will appear here and on the front page.{% if council.meeting.venueNote %}
  {{ council.meeting.venueNote }}{% endif %}</p>
  {% endif %}

  <ul class="fact-list">
    <li>Meetings are open to the public — anyone can come along, listen, and
    raise a point.</li>
    <li>Something for the agenda? Ask for it to be raised{% if council.ccContact %} —
    <a href="mailto:{{ council.ccContact }}">{{ council.ccContact }}</a>{% else %} —
    contact details will appear here once confirmed{% endif %}.</li>
    <li>Community councils run on volunteers and almost always have room for
    more. If you can give an evening a month, you'd be welcome — ask at any
    meeting.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

{# ================= WARD COUNCILLORS ================= #}
<section class="wrap council-page" aria-labelledby="ward-h">
  <h2 id="ward-h">Your North Ayrshire councillors</h2>
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <p class="page-intro">Largs sits in the <strong>{{ councillors.ward }}</strong> ward,
  which elects {{ councillors.seats }} councillors — and all {{ councillors.seats }}
  represent you. There's no "your" councillor by street or by party: write to any
  of them, or to all of them.</p>

  <div class="councillor-grid">
    {% for c in councillors.members %}
    <div class="councillor">
      <div class="name">{{ c.name }}</div>
      <div class="party">{{ c.party }}</div>
      {% if c.email %}<a class="clink" href="mailto:{{ c.email }}">{{ c.email }}</a>{% endif %}
      {% if c.phone %}<a class="clink" href="tel:{{ c.phone | replace(" ", "") }}">{{ c.phone }}</a>{% endif %}
      {% if c.surgeries %}<div class="clink">Surgeries: {{ c.surgeries }}</div>{% endif %}
      {% if c.profileUrl %}
      <a class="clink" href="{{ c.profileUrl }}">Official profile →</a>
      {% else %}
      <a class="clink" href="{{ councillors.nacListUrl }}">Contact details: NAC website →</a>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  <p class="verified-note">Roster checked {{ councillors.verified | dateLong }}.
  Party labels can change mid-term — the council's own website is the
  authoritative record, especially after any election.</p>
</section>

{# ================= REPORT IT RIGHT ================= #}
<section class="wrap council-page council-last" aria-labelledby="report-h">
  <h2 id="report-h">Report it right</h2>
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <p class="page-intro">Most problems have a proper front door, and it's rarely
  a councillor's inbox. Reported straight to the right desk, a fault gets
  logged, tracked and fixed sooner. And if the front door fails — that's
  exactly what your councillors are for.</p>

  {% for r in reportit.routes %}
  <div class="route-row">
    <div class="problem">{{ r.problem }}</div>
    <div class="route">{{ r.route }}
      {% if r.tel %}<a class="mono" href="tel:{{ r.tel | replace(" ", "") }}">{{ r.tel }}</a>{% endif %}
      {% if r.url %}<a href="{{ r.url }}">{{ r.urlLabel }} →</a>{% endif %}
    </div>
  </div>
  {% endfor %}
</section>
